p.src:
Taking the continuum limit gives the field Euler–Lagrange equation, $\boxed{
\partial_\mu
\left[
\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}
{\partial(\partial_\mu\phi)}
\right]
-
\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\phi}
=0
}.$ Repeated $\mu$ is summed over all spacetime directions. Written without the summation shorthand, the same equation is $\begin{aligned}&
\frac{\partial}{\partial t}
\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\phi_t}\right)
+
\frac{\partial}{\partial x}
\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\phi_x}\right)
+
\frac{\partial}{\partial y}
\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\phi_y}\right)
\nonumber\\
&\hspace{3em}
+
\frac{\partial}{\partial z}
\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\phi_z}\right)
-
\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\phi}
=0.
\end{aligned}$ The first term is the familiar particle term. Field theory adds an analogous term for every spatial direction. The last term, $-\partial\mathcal{L}/\partial\phi$, is unchanged.

