p.src:
Expanding the total time derivative gives $\begin{aligned}&(m-\phi)
\frac{d}{dt}
\left(
\frac{\dot x}{\sqrt{1-\dot x^2}}
\right)
-
\dot\phi\,
\frac{\dot x}{\sqrt{1-\dot x^2}}
\nonumber\\
&\hspace{10em}
=
\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial x}
\sqrt{1-\dot x^2}.
\end{aligned}$ If the background is static, $\phi=\phi(x)$, its derivative along the moving worldline is not zero. The chain rule gives $\boxed{
\dot\phi
=
\frac{d}{dt}\phi(x(t))
=
\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial x}\dot x
}.$

